  <h3>Rating Differentials Are Predictive</h3>
  <p>The correlation between rating differential and higher-rated player wins (r = <strong>{corr_diff_win}</strong>) confirms that even modest rating gaps translate into measurable win-probability shifts. A 100-point gap yields roughly a 55-58% win rate for the stronger player; at 300+ points, the probability climbs above 65%. Coaches should note that upsets are the exception, not the norm.</p>

  <h3>Resignation Dominates, but Checkmate Rates Vary by Time Control</h3>
  <p>Overall, <strong>{resign_rate}%</strong> of games end by resignation vs. <strong>{checkmate_rate}%</strong> by checkmate. However, in bullet/ultra-bullet formats, timeouts surge and checkmate rates drop, while longer time controls see checkmate climb above the overall average — players with more time convert advantages to mate rather than accepting resignation.</p>

  <h3>Opening Choice Matters — Some Defenses Favor Black</h3>
  <p>While white holds the aggregate edge, specific openings reverse the trend. Among openings with 50+ games, several ECO codes show black winning more often than white, suggesting that theoretically sound defenses can neutralize or even reverse white's first-move advantage when played by prepared opponents.</p>

  <h3>Game Depth Correlates with Player Strength</h3>
  <p>Expert-tier games (1800+ average rating) average noticeably more turns than beginner games, indicating that stronger players navigate deeper into endgames rather than falling to early tactical blows. Opening depth (number of book moves) also correlates with game length and outcome distributions.</p>

  <h3>Time Control Shapes Game Character</h3>
  <p>Bullet games are chaotic — shorter, timeout-heavy, with a slightly different white-advantage profile. Classical games are more "chess-like": longer, more draws, higher checkmate rates, and a more pronounced rating effect. Players choosing time controls should understand these structural differences when interpreting their own statistics.</p>
